IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc12-part9-duties.l4 -- the regulative rules (PARTY ... MUST / MAY ...) of Part IX, sections 321 to 378, over the core's `Party`.
-- Each rule takes, as an INPUT, the answer of the decision function of the same provision (a MAYBE BOOLEAN: JUST TRUE where the facts bring the case within
-- the provision), and says who must or may do what. NO deadline, sanction or remedy is invented: the text states none for any of these duties, so no rule has a WITHIN.
-- A MUST carries `LEST BREACH BECAUSE "<what was not done>"`, which names the omission and nothing more; a MAY has no LEST.
-- On the stable binary a DEONTIC value cannot be compared with EQUALS in an #ASSERT (`eval/unsupported-equality`), so the rules are run with #TRACE and the
-- same duties are asserted through the decision functions in ccc12-part9-*.l4 ("What the stable binary does not accept" in NOTES.md).
-- The duty-bearer for "every one who sells ..." is `the accused` of the core (the individual in the seat who may be liable); the court is `the court`.

§ `Regulative rules (the duties and powers of Part IX)`

§§ `ss 333.11 and 346.1 -- consecutive sentences`

DECLARE `s 333.11 action` IS ONE OF
    `s 333.11 order that the sentence be served consecutively to the other sentence`

-- src:6605 | Sentences to be served consecutively — breaking and entering
-- src:6606 | 333.11 (1) A sentence imposed on a person for an offence under subsection 333.1(3) or (4) shall be served consecutively to any other sentence imposed on the person for an offence under section 348 arising out of the same event or series of events.
@ref Criminal Code s 333.11(1) duty (src:6605-6606)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 333.11(1) the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 333.11 action`
`s 333.11(1) the court must order that the sentence be served consecutively` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 333.11(1) the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 333.11 order that the sentence be served consecutively to the other sentence`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the sentence for an offence under subsection 333.1(3) or (4) was not ordered to be served consecutively to the sentence for the section 348 offence from the same event or series of events"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:6607 | Sentences to be served consecutively — other offences
-- src:6608 | (2) A sentence imposed on a person for a second or subsequent offence under subsection 333.1(3) or (4) shall be served consecutively to any other sentence imposed on the person for an offence arising out of the same event or series of events.
@ref Criminal Code s 333.11(2) duty (src:6607-6608)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 333.11(2) the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 333.11 action`
`s 333.11(2) the court must order that the sentence be served consecutively` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 333.11(2) the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 333.11 order that the sentence be served consecutively to the other sentence`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the sentence for a second or subsequent offence under subsection 333.1(3) or (4) was not ordered to be served consecutively to the other sentence from the same event or series of events"
    ELSE FULFILLED

DECLARE `s 346.1 action` IS ONE OF
    `s 346.1 order that the sentence be served consecutively to the other sentence`

-- src:6794 | Sentences to be served consecutively
-- src:6795 | 346.1 A sentence imposed on a person for an offence under section 346 shall be served consecutively to any other sentence imposed on the person for an offence under any of sections 433 to 436 arising out of the same event or series of events.
@ref Criminal Code s 346.1 duty (src:6794-6795)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 346.1 the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 346.1 action`
`s 346.1 the court must order that the sentence be served consecutively` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 346.1 the sentence shall be served consecutively to the other sentence` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 346.1 order that the sentence be served consecutively to the other sentence`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the sentence for an offence under section 346 was not ordered to be served consecutively to the sentence for an offence under any of sections 433 to 436 from the same event or series of events"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `ss 334.1, 346(1.4) and 348.1 -- aggravating circumstances the court must consider`

DECLARE `s 334.1 action` IS ONE OF
    `s 334.1(1) consider as an aggravating circumstance the intent to sell or barter the stolen property or to fraudulently return it to a retail merchant`
    `s 334.1(2) consider as an aggravating circumstance the interference with an essential infrastructure`

-- src:6629 | Aggravating circumstance — stolen property
-- src:6630 | 334.1 (1) Without limiting the generality of section 718.2, when a court imposes a sentence for an offence referred to in section 322, 343, 348, 351 or 354, it shall consider as an aggravating circumstance the fact that the offence was committed with intent to sell or barter the stolen property or to fraudulently return it to a retail merchant.
@ref Criminal Code s 334.1(1) duty (src:6629-6630)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 334.1(1) the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 334.1 action`
`s 334.1(1) the court must consider the intent to sell or barter as an aggravating circumstance` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 334.1(1) the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 334.1(1) consider as an aggravating circumstance the intent to sell or barter the stolen property or to fraudulently return it to a retail merchant`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the court did not consider as an aggravating circumstance the intent to sell or barter the stolen property or to fraudulently return it to a retail merchant"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:6631 | Aggravating circumstance — essential infrastructure
-- src:6632 | (2) Without limiting the generality of section 718.2, when a court imposes a sentence for an offence referred to in section 322, 343 or 430, it shall consider as an aggravating circumstance the fact that the offence interfered with the access to or functioning of an essential infrastructure, as defined in subsection 52.1(2), or caused it to be unsafe or unfit for use.
@ref Criminal Code s 334.1(2) duty (src:6631-6632)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 334.1(2) the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 334.1 action`
`s 334.1(2) the court must consider the interference with an essential infrastructure as an aggravating circumstance` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 334.1(2) the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 334.1(2) consider as an aggravating circumstance the interference with an essential infrastructure`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the court did not consider as an aggravating circumstance that the offence interfered with an essential infrastructure or caused it to be unsafe or unfit for use"
    ELSE FULFILLED

DECLARE `s 346(1.4) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 346(1.4) consider as an aggravating factor that the offence was of a sexual nature or committed for a sexual purpose`

-- src:6790 | Aggravating factor
-- src:6791 | (1.4) If a person has been determined by a court to be guilty of an offence under this section, the court that imposes the sentence must consider as an aggravating factor the fact that the offence was of a sexual nature or committed for a sexual purpose.
@ref Criminal Code s 346(1.4) duty (src:6790-6791)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 346(1.4) the court must consider as an aggravating factor` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 346(1.4) action`
`s 346(1.4) the court must consider the sexual nature of the offence as an aggravating factor` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 346(1.4) the court must consider as an aggravating factor` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 346(1.4) consider as an aggravating factor that the offence was of a sexual nature or committed for a sexual purpose`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the court did not consider as an aggravating factor that the offence was of a sexual nature or committed for a sexual purpose"
    ELSE FULFILLED

DECLARE `s 348.1 action` IS ONE OF
    `s 348.1 consider as an aggravating circumstance that the dwelling-house was occupied`

-- src:6872 | Aggravating circumstance — home invasion
-- src:6873 | 348.1 If a person is convicted of an offence under section 98 or 98.1, subsection 279(2) or section 343, 346 or 348 in relation to a dwelling-house, the court imposing the sentence on the person shall consider as an aggravating circumstance the fact that the dwelling-house was occupied at the time of the commission of the offence and that the person, in committing the offence,
-- src:6874 | (a) knew that or was reckless as to whether the dwelling-house was occupied; and
-- src:6875 | (b) used violence or threats of violence to a person or property.
@ref Criminal Code s 348.1 duty (src:6872-6875)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 348.1 the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 348.1 action`
`s 348.1 the court must consider the occupation of the dwelling-house as an aggravating circumstance` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 348.1 the court shall consider as an aggravating circumstance` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MUST `s 348.1 consider as an aggravating circumstance that the dwelling-house was occupied`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the court did not consider as an aggravating circumstance that the dwelling-house was occupied and that the person knew or was reckless as to that and used violence or threats of violence"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `s 353(3) -- the record of a sale of an automobile master key`

DECLARE `s 353(3) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 353(3)(a) keep a record of the transaction showing the name and address of the purchaser and particulars of the licence issued to the purchaser`
    `s 353(3)(b) request the record for inspection`
    `s 353(3)(b) produce the record for inspection`

-- src:6908 | Record to be kept
-- src:6909 | (3) Every one who sells an automobile master key
-- src:6910 | (a) shall keep a record of the transaction showing the name and address of the purchaser and particulars of the licence issued to the purchaser as described in paragraph (1)(b); and
@ref Criminal Code s 353(3)(a) duty (src:6908-6910)
GIVEN `the person sells an automobile master key, by s 353(3) the person shall keep a record of the transaction and produce it for inspection` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 353(3) action`
`s 353(3)(a) the seller must keep a record of the transaction` MEANS
    IF `the person sells an automobile master key, by s 353(3) the person shall keep a record of the transaction and produce it for inspection` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the accused`
         MUST `s 353(3)(a) keep a record of the transaction showing the name and address of the purchaser and particulars of the licence issued to the purchaser`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the seller did not keep a record of the transaction"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- (b): the peace officer's request is the event that makes the duty to produce operate; the text states no time within which a peace officer may ask or the seller must produce.
-- src:6911 | (b) shall produce the record for inspection at the request of a peace officer.
@ref Criminal Code s 353(3)(b) duty (src:6911)
GIVEN `the person sells an automobile master key, by s 353(3) the person shall keep a record of the transaction and produce it for inspection` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 353(3) action`
`s 353(3)(b) the seller must produce the record for inspection at the request of a peace officer` MEANS
    IF `the person sells an automobile master key, by s 353(3) the person shall keep a record of the transaction and produce it for inspection` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `a peace officer`
         MAY `s 353(3)(b) request the record for inspection`
         HENCE PARTY `the accused`
               MUST `s 353(3)(b) produce the record for inspection`
               HENCE FULFILLED
               LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the seller did not produce the record for inspection at the request of a peace officer"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `ss 347.1(3) and (4) -- designation of a province for payday loans`

DECLARE `s 347.1 action` IS ONE OF
    `s 347.1(3) designate the province by order`
    `s 347.1(4) revoke the designation by order`

-- src:6844 | Designation of province
-- src:6845 | (3) The Governor in Council shall, by order and at the request of the lieutenant governor in council of a province, designate the province for the purposes of this section if the province has legislative measures that protect recipients of payday loans and that provide for limits on the total cost of borrowing under the agreements.
@ref Criminal Code s 347.1(3) duty (src:6844-6845)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 347.1(3) the Governor in Council shall designate the province` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 347.1 action`
`s 347.1(3) the Governor in Council must designate the province` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 347.1(3) the Governor in Council shall designate the province` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the Governor in Council`
         MUST `s 347.1(3) designate the province by order`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the Governor in Council did not designate the province on the request of its lieutenant governor in council"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:6846 | Revocation
-- src:6847 | (4) The Governor in Council shall, by order, revoke the designation made under subsection (3) if requested to do so by the lieutenant governor in council of the province or if the legislative measures described in that subsection are no longer in force in that province.
@ref Criminal Code s 347.1(4) duty (src:6846-6847)
GIVEN `the duty arises, by s 347.1(4) the Governor in Council shall revoke the designation` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 347.1 action`
`s 347.1(4) the Governor in Council must revoke the designation` MEANS
    IF `the duty arises, by s 347.1(4) the Governor in Council shall revoke the designation` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the Governor in Council`
         MUST `s 347.1(4) revoke the designation by order`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BECAUSE "the Governor in Council did not revoke the designation"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `Powers: forfeiture, entry, cross-examination`

DECLARE `s 327(2) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 327(2) order the device forfeited to Her Majesty`

-- src:6567 | Forfeiture
-- src:6568 | (2) If a person is convicted of an offence under subsection (1) or paragraph 326(1)(b), in addition to any punishment that is imposed, any device in relation to which the offence was committed or the possession of which constituted the offence may be ordered forfeited to Her Majesty and may be disposed of as the Attorney General directs.
@ref Criminal Code s 327(2) power (src:6567-6568)
GIVEN `the power exists, by s 327(2) the device may be ordered forfeited to Her Majesty` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 327(2) action`
`s 327(2) the court may order the device forfeited to Her Majesty` MEANS
    IF `the power exists, by s 327(2) the device may be ordered forfeited to Her Majesty` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY `s 327(2) order the device forfeited to Her Majesty`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

DECLARE `s 339(3) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 339(3) enter into or on the place to ascertain whether the lumber is detained there`

-- src:6665 | Search for timber unlawfully detained
-- src:6666 | (3) A peace officer who suspects, on reasonable grounds, that any lumber owned by any person and bearing the registered timber mark of that person is kept or detained in or on any place without the knowledge or consent of that person, may enter into or on that place to ascertain whether or not it is detained there without the knowledge or consent of that person.
@ref Criminal Code s 339(3) power (src:6665-6666)
GIVEN `the power exists, by s 339(3) the peace officer may enter into or on the place to ascertain whether the lumber is detained there` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 339(3) action`
`s 339(3) the peace officer may enter the place` MEANS
    IF `the power exists, by s 339(3) the peace officer may enter into or on the place to ascertain whether the lumber is detained there` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN PARTY `a peace officer`
         MAY `s 339(3) enter into or on the place to ascertain whether the lumber is detained there`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

DECLARE `s 347(6) action` IS ONE OF
    `s 347(6) require the attendance of the actuary for the purposes of cross-examination`

-- src:6817 | Cross-examination with leave
-- src:6818 | (6) An accused or a defendant against whom a certificate referred to in subsection (4) is produced may, with leave of the court, require the attendance of the actuary for the purposes of cross-examination.
@ref Criminal Code s 347(6) power (src:6817-6818)
